<p>Does this actually qualify as an antimeme? My understanding was that antimemes cause those affected by them to forget about the antimeme itself. In this case, it seems like those affected can still remember the specifics of the antimeme. Mnestics, the typical counter to antimemes, would be useless here, because remembering the anomaly doesn't help you reveal its properties. This seems more like a cognitohazard whose hazardous property just happens to be suppression of the ability/desire to communicate about the hazard itself.</p>
<p>It kinda makes me want to explore the idea of a cognitohazard whose hazard is that it forces those affected to act as if they don't remember the hazard, even if they do. The antimemetics division would probably have a hard time with that one, too.</p>

